Colour from tiny structures is called structural colour. It's how a peacock's tail, a butterfly's wing and a soap bubble get their shimmering colours, without blue or green pigment. A [[@soap-bubbles-colours|soap bubble]] changes colour as its film gets thinner, and a chameleon's crystals do something similar as the spacing changes.

Chameleons also have a deeper layer of crystals, bigger and less ordered, which reflects [[infrared|infrared]] light. Scientists think it may help keep them from overheating in strong sun.

{{story:power-cuttlefish}}

Most of a red panda's diet is bamboo leaves and young shoots, with some fruit, acorns, eggs and insects. Bamboo is not a great food. It is tough, and the red panda's stomach can only digest a small part of it.

So red pandas spend many hours a day eating, and save energy the rest of the time. They move slowly and sleep a lot. In very cold weather they can even lower how fast their body burns energy, a bit like turning down the heater in a house.

{{story:northeast-india}}

Most portable extinguishers have the same basic parts: a strong steel or aluminium cylinder, a valve with a handle and a hose or nozzle. A safety pin stops the handle being squeezed by accident.

In a stored-pressure extinguisher, the powder or water shares the can with squashed nitrogen or air. Pull the pin, squeeze the [[lever|lever]], and the valve opens. The gas pushes everything out through the nozzle in a jet. A carbon dioxide extinguisher is different. It holds CO₂ squeezed so hard that it turns liquid, and it comes out as a freezing white cloud. That is why it has a wide horn and why you should never touch the horn while using it.

