The pitch in the middle is made of rolled clay [[@soil-made|soil]] and very short grass. On day one it is usually firm and slightly damp, and fast bowlers can make the ball bounce and [[@cricket-swing|swing]].

As the days pass, the sun dries the pitch, the surface cracks and bowlers' footmarks rough it up. By days four and five, the ball can grip the surface and turn sharply, helping [[@cricket-spin|spin bowlers]]. A team batting last often faces the hardest conditions. Captains must plan for all five days at once.

{{story:soil-made}}

Starting a fire takes skill. One way uses [[@friction|friction]]: spinning a hard stick fast against a softer piece of wood until the dust gets hot enough to glow. Tiny embers are then dropped into dry grass and blown into flame. A bow, like a small archer's bow, makes the spinning easier.

The other way strikes certain stones together, such as flint against a mineral called pyrite, to throw sparks onto dry tinder. Some flint tools left by [[@neanderthals|Neanderthals]] in France, about 50,000 years old, have marks that may come from exactly this.

