From 1860, the British brought thousands of Indians to Natal on contracts called indenture. They worked on [[@sugar|sugar]] plantations for little pay, under harsh rules. Many stayed after their contracts ended. Indian traders followed, mostly from Gujarat. By Gandhi's time there were tens of thousands of Indians, part of a growing [[@indian-diaspora|Indian diaspora]] around the world.

Laws kept them out of many places. Natal charged former workers who stayed on a heavy yearly tax of three pounds, several months' wages for many, and Indians in the Transvaal had to carry passes.

{{story:indian-diaspora}}

Scholars have listed roughly 400 different signs. That number is a clue. [[@words-alphabet-order|An alphabet like English]] needs only 26 letters, while Chinese uses thousands of characters. A script with a few hundred signs probably mixes signs for whole words with signs for sounds, as Egyptian [[@hieroglyphs|hieroglyphs]] did.

Some signs crowd together at the left edge of a seal, as if the carver ran out of room. This suggests the writing usually ran from right to left. In 1977 the Indian scholar Iravatham Mahadevan published a huge list of every known inscription and sign, which researchers still use today.

{{story:cipher-codes}}

Disney had spent years making cartoons with his [[@animation-studios|animation studio]], and he planned the park the way a film director plans scenes. Visitors enter through a single gate onto Main Street, which leads straight to Sleeping Beauty Castle. From a central hub, paths fan out to different "lands".

Designers used a trick called forced perspective. Upper floors of buildings on Main Street are built smaller than the ground floor, so they look taller than they really are. Rubbish bins, trains and even staff costumes match each land. The aim was that you would never see a piece of the ordinary world from inside.

