Stand at the top in summer and the stone can be too hot to touch. Climb down and the air turns cool and damp. Visitors often measure the bottom at about 5°C cooler than the top.

That made the baori a gathering place. Families rested on the shady landings, and travellers stopped here on hot days. On the north side, a building with arched galleries and carved rooms looks down over the water. Historians think these rooms were added centuries after the well was dug.

{{story:groundwater}}

If you put Kannada and Telugu writing side by side, they look strikingly alike. That is because they grew from the same [[script|script]], sometimes called the Telugu-Kannada script, which itself came from the ancient Brahmi script used in Emperor [[@ashoka|Ashoka]]'s time.

For centuries, kingdoms such as the [[@chalukyas|Chalukyas]] wrote both languages in this shared script. Around the 13th and 14th centuries, the two began to drift apart into separate forms. Each is an [[abugida|abugida]]: every consonant has a built-in vowel, changed by adding small marks. The rounded shapes probably developed because early writers scratched letters onto palm leaves, where straight lines could split the leaf along its grain.

{{story:vijayanagara}}

Your brain doesn't store handwriting as a picture of each letter. It stores it as a plan of movements. Scientists call this a motor programme. When you write, your brain sends that plan to the muscles in your arm, hand and fingers.

The strange thing is that the plan isn't tied to one hand. Researchers have found that people writing with their other hand, a foot or a pen in their mouth still produce letters with similar shapes and slant to their usual handwriting. The writing is clumsier, but it is recognisably theirs. This shows how much of writing happens in the [[@brain-works|brain]], not just the hand.

{{story:brain-works}}
